1
00:00:01,000 --> 00:00:03,270
A little after eight in the evening.

2
00:00:03,350 --> 00:00:07,252
About ten thousand feet above the forests
of southwest Washington.

3
00:00:07,332 --> 00:00:10,248
In the cockpit,
the crew feel a small jolt.

4
00:00:10,328 --> 00:00:11,770
The tail lifts.

5
00:00:11,850 --> 00:00:15,129
At the back of the plane,
the stairs are open to the rain.

6
00:00:15,209 --> 00:00:17,824
And the man who opened them is gone.

7
00:00:17,904 --> 00:00:21,279
He has two hundred thousand dollars
strapped to his body.

8
00:00:21,359 --> 00:00:23,687
He's wearing a business suit and loafers.

9
00:00:23,767 --> 00:00:28,339
And for more than fifty years,
nobody has been able to say who he was.

10
00:00:35,589 --> 00:00:36,957
Portland, Oregon.

11
00:00:37,037 --> 00:00:39,601
The day before Thanksgiving,
nineteen seventy-one.

12
00:00:39,681 --> 00:00:43,257
A man in a dark suit buys
a one-way ticket to Seattle.

13
00:00:43,337 --> 00:00:44,730
A thirty-minute flight.

14
00:00:44,810 --> 00:00:46,056
He pays cash.

15
00:00:46,136 --> 00:00:48,428
He gives his name as Dan Cooper.

16
00:00:48,508 --> 00:00:50,237
Keep that name in mind.

17
00:00:50,317 --> 00:00:52,077
It's our first clue.

18
00:00:52,157 --> 00:00:54,360
And it's wrong in more ways than one.

19
00:00:55,610 --> 00:00:57,399
He orders a bourbon and soda.

20
00:00:57,479 --> 00:00:58,842
Lights a cigarette.

21
00:00:58,922 --> 00:01:02,147
Just after takeoff,
he hands a flight attendant a folded note.

22
00:01:02,227 --> 00:01:06,709
She thinks it's his phone number,
and pockets it without reading it.

23
00:01:06,789 --> 00:01:08,137
He leans over.

24
00:01:08,250 --> 00:01:11,393
<i>Miss, you'd better look at that note.</i>

25
00:01:11,473 --> 00:01:12,862
<i>I have a bomb.</i>

26
00:01:14,070 --> 00:01:17,257
In his briefcase: red cylinders,
wires, a battery.

27
00:01:17,337 --> 00:01:20,590
He wants two hundred thousand dollars.

28
00:01:20,670 --> 00:01:24,210
Four parachutes.
And a fuel truck waiting in Seattle.

29
00:01:24,290 --> 00:01:28,179
The plane circles for two hours
while the FBI gathers the cash.

30
00:01:28,259 --> 00:01:30,417
Ten thousand twenty-dollar bills.

31
00:01:30,497 --> 00:01:34,806
And before they hand it over,
agents photograph every single one.

32
00:01:34,886 --> 00:01:36,400
Every serial number.

33
00:01:36,480 --> 00:01:38,352
Remember that, too.

34
00:01:39,602 --> 00:01:43,264
In Seattle, he trades the passengers
for the money and the parachutes.

35
00:01:43,344 --> 00:01:46,436
Then he gives the crew
a very specific flight plan.

36
00:01:46,516 --> 00:01:47,815
Head for Mexico.

37
00:01:47,895 --> 00:01:49,539
Fly low, below ten thousand feet.

38
00:01:49,619 --> 00:01:50,790
Landing gear down.

39
00:01:50,870 --> 00:01:52,068
Flaps lowered.

40
00:01:52,148 --> 00:01:54,873
As slow as the plane can go.

41
00:01:54,953 --> 00:01:56,528
And the rear stairs… open.

42
00:01:56,608 --> 00:01:59,260
It's an odd set of instructions.

43
00:01:59,340 --> 00:02:02,214
Not something most people
would think to ask for.

44
00:02:02,294 --> 00:02:04,901
Which brings us to clue number two.

45
00:02:06,493 --> 00:02:09,309
Seven-forty. They take off again.

46
00:02:09,389 --> 00:02:12,067
He sends the last flight
attendant to the cockpit.

47
00:02:12,147 --> 00:02:15,772
She looks back, and sees him
tying something around his waist.

48
00:02:15,852 --> 00:02:18,009
Not long after, a red light comes on.

49
00:02:18,089 --> 00:02:20,043
The stairs are down.

50
00:02:20,123 --> 00:02:22,425
A little after eight.

51
00:02:22,505 --> 00:02:24,260
The jolt.

52
00:02:24,340 --> 00:02:27,058
Two hours later, they land in Reno.

53
00:02:27,138 --> 00:02:28,566
The cabin is empty.

54
00:02:28,646 --> 00:02:31,069
He's taken the money and the briefcase.

55
00:02:31,149 --> 00:02:33,177
He's left behind two parachutes,

56
00:02:33,257 --> 00:02:36,628
a few cigarette butts,
and a black clip-on tie.

57
00:02:37,878 --> 00:02:41,607
That night, one of the longest
investigations in FBI history begins.

58
00:02:41,687 --> 00:02:44,232
And it starts with almost nothing.

59
00:02:44,312 --> 00:02:45,504
No face.

60
00:02:45,584 --> 00:02:46,837
No real name.

61
00:02:46,917 --> 00:02:49,811
No body. No parachute.

62
00:02:49,891 --> 00:02:52,879
Just a handful of clues.

63
00:02:52,959 --> 00:02:55,682
Let's go through them, one at a time.

64
00:02:56,932 --> 00:03:00,702
Police question a man in Portland
whose name really is D.B. Cooper.

65
00:03:00,782 --> 00:03:02,973
He has nothing to do with it.

66
00:03:03,053 --> 00:03:04,814
But a reporter hears the name,

67
00:03:04,894 --> 00:03:08,593
and the hijacker who called
himself Dan becomes "D.B." Cooper.

68
00:03:08,673 --> 00:03:10,639
A name that was never his.

69
00:03:10,719 --> 00:03:13,580
Years later,
investigators notice something else.

70
00:03:13,660 --> 00:03:17,052
In the nineteen-fifties
and sixties there was a popular

71
00:03:17,132 --> 00:03:20,315
comic book about a heroic
Canadian air force pilot.

72
00:03:20,395 --> 00:03:22,277
His name was Dan Cooper.

73
00:03:22,357 --> 00:03:24,633
The comic was published in French.

74
00:03:24,713 --> 00:03:25,924
Never in English.

75
00:03:26,004 --> 00:03:28,732
So did our man pick a random name?

76
00:03:28,812 --> 00:03:32,050
Or had he spent time in Europe
or Canada… and borrowed

77
00:03:32,130 --> 00:03:35,138
his alias from a hero
who parachutes out of planes?

78
00:03:36,388 --> 00:03:37,869
Look at what he asked for.

79
00:03:37,949 --> 00:03:39,903
Four parachutes, not one.

80
00:03:39,983 --> 00:03:42,729
Possibly so the FBI wouldn't
dare give him a faulty one,

81
00:03:42,809 --> 00:03:45,260
in case he took someone with him.

82
00:03:45,340 --> 00:03:48,162
A speed and flap setting
that suited a jump.

83
00:03:48,242 --> 00:03:49,794
And the rear stairs.

84
00:03:49,874 --> 00:03:52,236
The Boeing seven-twenty-seven
was one of the

85
00:03:52,316 --> 00:03:54,704
few airliners you could jump
from in flight.

86
00:03:54,784 --> 00:03:57,097
Very few people knew that.

87
00:03:57,177 --> 00:04:00,058
Some researchers point out
that the same type of plane had

88
00:04:00,138 --> 00:04:03,553
been used for secret parachute drops
in Southeast Asia.

89
00:04:03,633 --> 00:04:06,377
On the way,
he even recognised Tacoma from the air,

90
00:04:06,457 --> 00:04:08,934
and knew where the air force base was.

91
00:04:09,014 --> 00:04:11,878
This was not a random man
with a random plan.

92
00:04:11,958 --> 00:04:14,829
He knew planes. Maybe the military.

93
00:04:16,079 --> 00:04:17,621
Now picture the jump.

94
00:04:17,701 --> 00:04:19,937
At night. In rain.

95
00:04:20,017 --> 00:04:21,398
Into a freezing wind.

96
00:04:21,478 --> 00:04:23,726
Over dense forest, with no lights below.

97
00:04:23,806 --> 00:04:26,024
In a business suit.

98
00:04:26,104 --> 00:04:26,648
And loafers.

99
00:04:26,728 --> 00:04:31,752
The FBI agent who led the case,
Ralph Himmelsbach, was a pilot himself.

100
00:04:31,832 --> 00:04:34,304
He never believed Cooper survived.

101
00:04:34,384 --> 00:04:37,448
Neither did the agents who came after him.

102
00:04:37,528 --> 00:04:41,328
And one of the two parachutes he jumped
with was a training reserve.

103
00:04:41,408 --> 00:04:42,454
Sewn shut.

104
00:04:42,534 --> 00:04:45,479
Useless. An expert might have noticed.

105
00:04:45,559 --> 00:04:47,633
But here's the thing.

106
00:04:47,713 --> 00:04:50,580
Hundreds of soldiers
searched those woods, twice.

107
00:04:50,660 --> 00:04:52,887
Not one of them found a body.

108
00:04:52,967 --> 00:04:54,285
Or a parachute.

109
00:04:55,535 --> 00:04:57,533
Remember the serial numbers.

110
00:04:57,613 --> 00:05:01,862
The FBI sent the list to banks,
casinos and businesses across the country.

111
00:05:01,942 --> 00:05:05,798
If Cooper spent his money,
a bill should turn up somewhere.

112
00:05:05,878 --> 00:05:07,194
A store.

113
00:05:07,274 --> 00:05:09,892
A bank. A racetrack.

114
00:05:09,972 --> 00:05:12,730
As far as we know, not one ever did.

115
00:05:12,810 --> 00:05:16,598
For eight years, not a single bill
from the ransom was found anywhere.

116
00:05:16,678 --> 00:05:20,235
And then,
in nineteen eighty, some of it was.

117
00:05:21,485 --> 00:05:23,195
February, nineteen eighty.

118
00:05:23,275 --> 00:05:27,193
An eight-year-old boy is digging
on a sandy bank of the Columbia River.

119
00:05:27,273 --> 00:05:32,200
He finds bundles of old twenty-dollar
bills, rotting, still held together.

120
00:05:32,280 --> 00:05:34,895
Five thousand eight hundred dollars.

121
00:05:34,975 --> 00:05:37,185
And the serial numbers match.

122
00:05:37,265 --> 00:05:39,244
It should have solved the case.

123
00:05:39,324 --> 00:05:41,567
It made it stranger.

124
00:05:41,647 --> 00:05:43,969
The geologist
who studied the site believed

125
00:05:44,049 --> 00:05:46,185
the money arrived years
after the hijacking,

126
00:05:46,265 --> 00:05:48,578
above a layer of dredged sand.

127
00:05:48,658 --> 00:05:51,251
Others have since challenged that.

128
00:05:51,331 --> 00:05:55,038
And in twenty twenty, a scientist
found microscopic algae on the bills.

129
00:05:55,118 --> 00:05:57,175
A kind that blooms in spring.

130
00:05:57,255 --> 00:05:59,674
Not in November, when Cooper jumped.

131
00:05:59,754 --> 00:06:03,107
So how did a bundle
of Cooper's money end up in the river,

132
00:06:03,187 --> 00:06:06,170
months or years later…
and only part of it?

133
00:06:07,420 --> 00:06:10,057
The last clue is the one he left behind.

134
00:06:10,137 --> 00:06:11,442
The black tie.

135
00:06:11,522 --> 00:06:15,543
In two thousand one, the FBI pulled
a partial DNA profile from it.

136
00:06:15,623 --> 00:06:17,676
Then in the twenty-tens,

137
00:06:17,756 --> 00:06:22,541
a team of volunteer scientists looked
at the tie under an electron microscope.

138
00:06:22,621 --> 00:06:25,866
They found more
than a hundred thousand tiny particles.

139
00:06:25,946 --> 00:06:29,324
Including pure titanium,
and the rare earth metal cerium.

140
00:06:29,404 --> 00:06:33,665
In nineteen seventy-one,
those were used in only a few places.

141
00:06:33,745 --> 00:06:35,569
Like aerospace plants.

142
00:06:35,649 --> 00:06:38,618
Places where engineers wore ties to work.

143
00:06:38,698 --> 00:06:40,635
The FBI's answer was careful.

144
00:06:40,715 --> 00:06:43,719
They don't know for sure the tie was his.

145
00:06:44,969 --> 00:06:48,996
Over the years, the FBI looked
at more than eight hundred suspects.

146
00:06:49,076 --> 00:06:51,694
Five of them come up again and again.

147
00:06:51,774 --> 00:06:54,032
All of them have died.

148
00:06:54,112 --> 00:06:56,530
None was ever proven to be Cooper.

149
00:06:57,780 --> 00:07:01,921
Five months after Cooper,
a man hijacked another seven-twenty-seven,

150
00:07:02,001 --> 00:07:05,274
took half a million dollars,
and jumped out over Utah.

151
00:07:05,354 --> 00:07:06,486
Same method.

152
00:07:06,566 --> 00:07:07,819
Same stairs.

153
00:07:07,899 --> 00:07:09,207
Richard McCoy.

154
00:07:09,287 --> 00:07:11,736
A former soldier, a skydiver.

155
00:07:11,816 --> 00:07:13,527
He was caught within days.

156
00:07:13,607 --> 00:07:14,908
Perfect fit?

157
00:07:14,988 --> 00:07:16,404
The FBI said no.

158
00:07:16,484 --> 00:07:17,696
McCoy was twenty-nine,

159
00:07:17,776 --> 00:07:21,877
and he didn't match what the two
flight attendants described.

160
00:07:21,957 --> 00:07:24,596
Some researchers still argue about it.

161
00:07:26,107 --> 00:07:29,761
Kenneth Christiansen worked
for the very airline that was hijacked.

162
00:07:29,841 --> 00:07:31,506
He had been a paratrooper.

163
00:07:31,586 --> 00:07:36,305
About a year after the hijacking,
he bought a house, reportedly with cash.

164
00:07:36,385 --> 00:07:40,188
After he died,
his own brother came to suspect him.

165
00:07:40,268 --> 00:07:43,459
But he was shorter and slighter
than the man on the plane,

166
00:07:43,539 --> 00:07:46,472
and the FBI did not find
enough to name him.

167
00:07:47,722 --> 00:07:53,045
Duane Weber, on his deathbed, reportedly
told his wife: "I'm Dan Cooper."

168
00:07:53,125 --> 00:07:55,586
She didn't know what it meant.

169
00:07:55,666 --> 00:07:59,452
The FBI compared his DNA
with the profile from the tie.

170
00:07:59,532 --> 00:08:01,451
It didn't match.

171
00:08:02,701 --> 00:08:03,840
Robert Rackstraw.

172
00:08:03,920 --> 00:08:06,999
A Vietnam veteran with parachute training.

173
00:08:07,079 --> 00:08:11,053
Decades later, a team of amateur
investigators built a case around him,

174
00:08:11,133 --> 00:08:14,128
and he became one
of the best-known suspects of all.

175
00:08:14,208 --> 00:08:17,793
But in nineteen seventy-one
he was only twenty-eight.

176
00:08:17,873 --> 00:08:22,426
The FBI had questioned him in the
seventies, and dropped him as a suspect.

177
00:08:22,506 --> 00:08:24,321
He always denied it.

178
00:08:25,830 --> 00:08:28,634
And then there's the suspect
most investigators favour.

179
00:08:28,714 --> 00:08:31,291
A man whose name we've never heard.

180
00:08:31,371 --> 00:08:33,173
Who died that night in the forest.

181
00:08:33,253 --> 00:08:37,320
And who was never missed by anyone
who connected him to a hijacking.

182
00:08:37,400 --> 00:08:38,797
He fits every clue.

183
00:08:38,877 --> 00:08:41,163
Which is exactly the problem.

184
00:08:42,716 --> 00:08:44,245
So here are the endings.

185
00:08:44,325 --> 00:08:45,801
Pick one.

186
00:08:45,881 --> 00:08:47,574
One. He didn't make it.

187
00:08:47,654 --> 00:08:51,914
He landed in the dark,
in the cold, somewhere in that forest.

188
00:08:51,994 --> 00:08:53,460
And the forest kept him.

189
00:08:53,540 --> 00:08:54,708
Two.

190
00:08:54,788 --> 00:08:56,218
He made it.

191
00:08:56,298 --> 00:08:58,673
He walked out of the woods, hid the money,

192
00:08:58,753 --> 00:09:02,200
and lived a quiet life,
knowing something nobody else knew.

193
00:09:02,280 --> 00:09:03,432
Three.

194
00:09:03,512 --> 00:09:05,044
Something in between.

195
00:09:05,124 --> 00:09:08,368
He made it to the river, or the money did.

196
00:09:08,448 --> 00:09:10,740
And the river kept the rest.

197
00:09:10,820 --> 00:09:13,134
Every one of them fits some of the clues.

198
00:09:13,214 --> 00:09:14,670
None of them fits all.

199
00:09:16,242 --> 00:09:18,466
In twenty sixteen, after forty-five years,

200
00:09:18,546 --> 00:09:20,886
the FBI stopped actively investigating.

201
00:09:20,966 --> 00:09:25,164
It's still the only unsolved hijacking
in American commercial aviation.

202
00:09:25,244 --> 00:09:28,008
But the case board never really came down.

203
00:09:28,088 --> 00:09:30,390
People are still out there.

204
00:09:30,470 --> 00:09:32,135
Checking the river.

205
00:09:32,215 --> 00:09:33,518
Testing the tie.

206
00:09:33,598 --> 00:09:35,327
Reading the old files.

207
00:09:36,577 --> 00:09:38,855
Somewhere out there is the answer.

208
00:09:38,935 --> 00:09:41,157
A name. A grave.

209
00:09:41,237 --> 00:09:45,026
Or a bundle of twenty-dollar bills,
still waiting in the sand.

210
00:09:45,106 --> 00:09:47,617
He planned everything.

211
00:09:47,697 --> 00:09:50,114
Except one thing he couldn't control.

212
00:09:50,194 --> 00:09:53,064
Whether we'd ever stop looking.

